librecomp: end the process rather than race a live thread on quit

Closing the game's window called ultramodern::quit(), and a fraction of the time
that ended in a segfault instead of an exit. The crash was always in the game's
own recompiled code, a few instructions into a load, on a thread the shutdown
never touched.

A game keeps several OS threads of its own -- a sound thread, a retrace thread --
and the cooperative scheduler runs exactly one of them at a time while the rest
are parked on a semaphore. quit() sets the exit flag, the main loop breaks, and
the runtime joins the main game thread and its own service threads and then frees
rdram. It does not join the game's other threads, and it does not stop the one
that was running: that thread keeps executing recompiled code until it next
yields, which for a thread deep in an audio synthesis loop is not soon. When the
free landed first, the thread read rdram the kernel had just taken back. Which
thread was mid-run at quit is a matter of timing, so the crash came and went --
and a change to the frame pacing that shifted that timing was enough to make it
show every time.

There is nothing after the join worth that race. join_saving_thread has already
written the save to disk -- a write reaches the host-side buffer synchronously
when the game makes it, and that join is where the buffer is flushed -- so the
one piece of state a player cares about is durable before rdram is touched.
Freeing rdram, closing the audio device and unwinding main() are all things the
operating system does for us, correctly and instantly, when the process ends. So
the process ends, with std::_Exit, rather than unmapping memory a live thread is
still reading.

--- a/librecomp/src/recomp.cpp
+++ b/librecomp/src/recomp.cpp
@@ -1136,18 +1136,25 @@
     ultramodern::join_event_threads();
     ultramodern::join_thread_cleaner_thread();
     ultramodern::join_saving_thread();
-    
-    // Free rdram.
-    bool free_failed;
-#ifdef _WIN32
-    // VirtualFree returns zero on failure.
-    free_failed = (VirtualFree(rdram, 0, MEM_RELEASE) == 0);
-#else
-    // munmap returns -1 on failure.
-    free_failed = (munmap(rdram, allocation_size) == -1);
-#endif
-
-    if (free_failed) {
-        printf("Failed to free rdram\n");
-    }
+
+    // The game's own OS threads are not joined above -- only the main game
+    // thread and the runtime's service threads are. A game keeps several
+    // threads of its own (a sound thread, a retrace thread), and exactly one of
+    // them is running recompiled code at any instant while the rest are parked
+    // on a semaphore. `quit()` sets the exit flag but does not stop that running
+    // thread; it keeps executing until it next yields, which for a thread deep
+    // in a synthesis loop can be a while. Freeing rdram out from under it is a
+    // race with only one safe outcome and one segfault, and on window close the
+    // segfault is what a player got -- the running thread reading rdram a few
+    // instructions after `munmap` returned it to the kernel.
+    //
+    // There is nothing left to do that is worth that race. `join_saving_thread`
+    // above has already flushed the save to disk -- a write reaches the
+    // host-side buffer synchronously when the game makes it, and that join is
+    // where the buffer is written out -- so the only state a player cares about
+    // is durable before this point. Freeing rdram, closing the audio device and
+    // returning up through main() are all housekeeping the operating system does
+    // for us, correctly and instantly, the moment the process ends. So end it,
+    // rather than unmap memory a live thread is still reading.
+    std::_Exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
 }
